  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 306 AM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: The current weak midlevel troughing regime \ncontinues to break down this morning as an upstream split flow \npattern begins to build across most of the CONUS. As a result, a \nbroad ridging pattern occupies the northern U.S., while low \namplitude troughing keeps warm low-level temperatures south of the \nCWA. At the surface, high pressure begins to break down overnight, \ndelivering light rainfall and patchy morning fog, resulting in \nbeautiful fall weather this afternoon through the remainder of the \nweek. High temperatures remain around normal for mid to late \nSeptember, with most days expected to be in the 60s but cool \novernight lows will fall into the 40s and upper 30s. \n\nForecast Details: Conditions remain mostly cloudy today as surface \nlow pressure remains positioned over central Indiana. Showers are \nmostly expected to remain south of the CWA, but southernmost \ncounties could observe an additional trace of rainfall accompanied \nby some patchy morning fog.  Post-frontal pressure gradient force \nwinds will clear out low-level moisture and build breezy northeast \nwinds today. High pressure begins to build across the Great Lakes \nregion this Monday through the remainder of the week. This pattern \nwill support quiet and dry conditions through the next several days. \nH8 temperatures in the single digits will keep daytime highs in the \n60s. Only concerning weather to monitor will be sufficient overnight \nradiational cooling causing overnight lows to drop into the low 40s \nand upper 30s. Coldest night be Monday night where interior \nareas/elevated terrain will likely observe lows near freezing, \npotentially leading to frost. No precipitation potential is expected \nthrough the entirety of the forecast period.",
